SMB Brute-Force Attack & Wazuh Detection Lab

A hands-on cybersecurity lab demonstrating how an SMB password-guessing attack can be performed in an isolated Active Directory environment and detected using Wazuh SIEM.

«Lab environment: VirtualBox home lab
Purpose: Security testing, detection engineering, and SIEM validation
MITRE ATT&CK: T1110.001 — Password Guessing»

---

📌 Project Overview

This project simulates an SMB brute-force attack against a domain-joined Windows Server 2019 machine using Kali Linux.

The objective was not only to perform the attack, but also to follow the activity from the attacker machine to the Windows endpoint and finally into Wazuh.

During the exercise, I discovered that Windows auditing was initially disabled. This prevented failed authentication events from being generated and created a detection gap.

After enabling the appropriate Windows auditing policies, the attack generated Windows Security Event ID 4625, which was successfully collected by Wazuh and matched by custom detection rules.

