🔎 1. SMB Enumeration

The target SMB service was first tested using "smbclient".

smbclient -L //192.168.56.116 -U 'Hopelabs\HR-STAFF1'

The target returned standard Windows shares:

ADMIN$
C$
IPC$
Users

This confirmed that:

- The target was reachable.
- SMB was accessible.
- The account could authenticate.
- The SMB service was responding.

The SMB1 workgroup listing returned:

NT_STATUS_RESOURCE_NAME_NOT_FOUND

This did not prevent SMB authentication and was consistent with SMB1 being disabled on the modern Windows system.

---

🔐 2. SMB Authentication Testing

The first tool tested was THC-Hydra.

hydra -l HR-STAFF1 -p passwords.txt -t 1 192.168.56.116 smb

Hydra returned:

[ERROR] invalid reply from target

Additional SMB module and connection options were tested, but the authentication process did not complete successfully.

The lab was therefore continued using CrackMapExec, which successfully handled the SMB authentication process against the target.

---

🧪 3. Password-Guessing Simulation

CrackMapExec was used to test the credentials against the SMB service.

crackmapexec smb 192.168.56.116 -u HR-STAFF1 -p passwords.txt -d HopeLabs.Local

The test produced failed authentication responses followed by a successful authentication:

[-] HopeLabs.Local\HR-STAFF1:msfadmin    STATUS_LOGON_FAILURE
[-] HopeLabs.Local\HR-STAFF1:kali        STATUS_LOGON_FAILURE
[-] HopeLabs.Local\HR-STAFF1:vagrant     STATUS_LOGON_FAILURE
[-] HopeLabs.Local\HR-STAFF1:ubuntu      STATUS_LOGON_FAILURE
[+] HopeLabs.Local\HR-STAFF1:Hope199872

This confirmed that the test account was being authenticated through the domain and that incorrect passwords were generating authentication failures.

---

🛡️ 4. Wazuh Detection Investigation

After performing the authentication tests, the Windows Security logs were checked to determine whether the failed attempts were being recorded.

The Windows audit policy was checked using:

auditpol /get /category:"Logon/Logoff"

The relevant categories were initially configured as:

No Auditing

This explained why the expected authentication events were not appearing in Wazuh.

Detection Gap

The Wazuh agent was running and connected to the manager, but the endpoint was not generating the required Windows Security events.

This demonstrated an important SIEM troubleshooting principle:

No Event Generated
        ↓
Nothing to Forward
        ↓
Nothing for Wazuh to Detect

---

⚙️ 5. Enabling Windows Auditing

Successful and failed logon auditing was enabled:

auditpol /set /subcategory:"Logon" /success:enable /failure:enable

Logoff auditing was also enabled:

auditpol /set /subcategory:"Logoff" /success:enable /failure:enable

The configuration was verified:

auditpol /get /category:"Logon/Logoff"

The relevant categories now showed:

Success and Failure

---

🚨 6. Wazuh Detection

The authentication test was repeated after enabling Windows auditing.

Windows began generating:

Event ID: 4625

Event ID 4625 represents a failed logon attempt.

Wazuh successfully collected the event from the Windows Server and applied custom detection rules.

Custom Wazuh Rules

Rule ID| Level| Description
"100100"| 10| Windows Failed Login Detected
"100101"| 12| Multiple Windows Failed Logons Detected – Possible Brute Force

