🔍 10. Key Findings

Finding 1 — SMB Tool Compatibility

The initial SMB testing tool did not successfully complete authentication against the target. A different SMB testing tool was able to communicate with the target and perform the authentication tests.

Finding 2 — Domain Context

Providing the Active Directory domain improved authentication reliability:

-d HopeLabs.Local

Finding 3 — Logging Was Initially Disabled

The most important detection issue was discovered on the Windows endpoint.

The Wazuh agent was functioning, but Windows was not generating the required authentication events because auditing was disabled.

Finding 4 — Wazuh Detection Worked After Logging Was Enabled

Once Windows auditing was configured correctly:

Authentication Attempt
        ↓
Windows Event 4625
        ↓
Wazuh Agent
        ↓
Wazuh Manager
        ↓
Custom Detection Rule
        ↓
Alert

The complete detection pipeline worked as expected.

---

📚 Lessons Learned

This lab reinforced several practical SOC concepts:

- SIEM detection depends on endpoint telemetry.
- An agent being connected does not guarantee that useful security events are being generated.
- Windows audit policies should be checked when authentication events are missing.
- Event ID 4625 is useful for detecting failed Windows authentication.
- Source IP addresses can help identify the system generating authentication attempts.
- Logon Type can provide useful context about how authentication occurred.
- Repeated authentication failures can be correlated into a brute-force detection.
- Detection engineering requires validating both the attack and the logging pipeline.

